        "problem": "Find ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{5}+1}.$",
        "worked_solution": "First we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{5}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (5{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
